{"qid":"Q241748","names":["Kingdom of Serbia"],"title":"Kingdom of Serbia","endonym":null,"capsule":"The modern Kingdom of Serbia was formally proclaimed in 1882 when Prince Milan Obrenović elevated his title to king following the Congress of Berlin's recognition of Serbian independence in 1878. The state expanded through the Balkan Wars of 1912–1913, gaining Macedonia and Kosovo, and entered World War I in 1914 after the assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand; despite catastrophic losses in the Great Retreat across Albania in 1915–1916, Serbian forces regrouped at Corfu and returned to the Salonika front, contributing to the Allied breakthrough in 1918 that led to the formation of the Kingdom of Serbs, Croats and Slovenes. The 1188 attestation from date reflects the earlier medieval kingdom's continuity as used in the Cliopatria dataset.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. The modern Kingdom of Serbia's borders (1878–1918) are well defined by international treaties; the dataset's 1188 start date reflects the medieval kingdom's attestation and the territorial extents for the medieval period are less precisely bounded.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Serbia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Serbia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Serbia (medieval)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Serbia_(medieval)"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Great Retreat (Serbia)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Great_Retreat_(Serbia)"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Serbia","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Stefan Nemanja","nativeName":"Стефан Немања","rom